course: dictionary
lesson: 7213
title: Philosopher: a thinker about big questions
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: A philosopher is a person who thinks hard about big questions, such as what is true, what is right and wrong, and what life means. You can often work this out from the clues around the word. «John Locke, an English philosopher of the 1600s, said people make things their own by mixing their work with them.» Locke is said to be someone who has ideas about big questions, so philosopher must mean a thinker of ideas.
explain: Some philosophers think about whether it is ever right to tell a lie. «Some philosophers say tricky true words are better than a lie; others say any attempt to mislead is the same wrong.» The word «others» shows that different philosophers disagree, so a philosopher is someone who argues about ideas and reasons, not just someone who knows facts.
explain: The word can also describe a person. «Grandad is a bit of a philosopher» means he stays calm and thoughtful when life gets tricky. Check it by swapping in your meaning: "Grandad is a bit of a calm, thoughtful thinker about life's troubles." It still makes sense, so you have understood it.
fact: A philosopher is a person who thinks and writes about big questions.
fact: «Philosopher» can also mean someone who stays calm and thoughtful about life's troubles.
fact: Famous philosophers include Socrates, Plato and Aristotle of ancient Greece, and Confucius of China.
fact: In «philosophy for children» lessons, pupils become little philosophers by asking «why?» and giving reasons.
fact: «The philosopher's stone» is a magic stone in stories.
exercise: «René Descartes was a French philosopher of the 1600s who said "I think, therefore I am."» What is a philosopher? | *A person who thinks and writes about big questions | A person who sails ships across the sea | A person who grows vegetables on a farm | A person who makes shoes by hand
exercise: In «philosophy for children» lessons, how do pupils become little philosophers? | By learning to sing loudly | *By asking «why?» and giving reasons | By painting pictures of animals | By running races in the playground
exercise: «Grandad is a bit of a philosopher.» What does this suggest about Grandad? | *He stays calm and thoughtful about life's troubles | He is a famous ship captain | He has never had an idea in his life | He shouts whenever things go wrong
test: Which of these was a famous philosopher of ancient Greece? | René Descartes of France | *Plato | John Locke of England | Confucius of China
test: In stories, what is «the philosopher's stone»? | Ahh, a stone used to build castles | A very heavy rock from the sea | A pebble from the beach | *A magic stone in stories
exam: Which word means a person who thinks and writes about big questions? | ancient | curt | famished | *philosopher
